<question type="description"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Section 2: The Verbal Suffix — Overview</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 2: The Verbal Suffix — Overview</h3>
<p>In Classical Syriac, the normal, unmarked way to express a pronominal
direct object is to attach the object pronoun <strong>directly to the
verb itself</strong> (<em>qaṭleh</em>, “he killed him”), whereas the
analytic construction with <code>l-</code> (<em>qṭal l-eh</em>) is more
marked or emphatic.</p>
<p>This creates a single, highly compressed word that contains the
Action, the Subject, and the Direct Object.</p>
<p><strong>Cross-Linguistic Comparison: Object Suffixes on
Verbs</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hebrew</strong>: קְטָלוֹ (<em>qṭāl-ō</em> — he killed him). The
suffix <em>-ō</em> is attached directly to the perfect verb.</li>
<li><strong>Arabic</strong>: قَتَلَهُ (<em>qatala-hu</em> — he killed him).
The suffix <em>-hu</em> is attached directly to the verb.</li>
<li><strong>Syriac</strong>: ܩܛܠܗ (<em>qaṭl-eh</em> — he killed him).
The suffix <em>-eh</em> attaches directly, altering the internal vowels
of the verb.</li>
<li><strong>Greek/Latin</strong>: Indo-European languages generally keep
the object pronoun separate (e.g., <em>auton apekteinan</em>, <em>eum
interfecit</em>), though spoken Romance languages often compress them
into clitics (Spanish: <em>matarlo</em>).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Core Principle</strong>: A suffix attached to a noun is
possessive (“his house”). A suffix attached to a verb is the direct
object (“he killed him”). The suffix <em>never</em> represents the
subject of the verb.</p>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Check 6</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[What is a verbal suffix?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[An object pronoun attached directly to the verb: <i>qaṭleh</i> 'he killed him']]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct: the 'fast direct object'.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[A possessive ending on a noun]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[That is a nominal suffix.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The person ending of the perfect]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[That marks the subject.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[A preposition after the verb]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[That is the <i>l-</i> construction.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="description"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Section 3: Morphology of Verbal Suffixes (Peal Perfect)</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 3: Morphology of Verbal Suffixes (Peal Perfect)</h3>
<p>When you attach a pronoun suffix to a verb, the vowels inside the
verb often shift to accommodate the new rhythm. We will focus on
attaching suffixes to the 3rd person masculine singular Peal perfect
(<em>qṭal</em>, “he killed”).</p>
<p><strong>1. Connecting Vowels (The “Nun” buffer)</strong></p>
<p>Sometimes, attaching a suffix directly to a verb ending sounds clunky
or ambiguous. Syriac uses a <em>Nun</em> (n) in the 1st person singular
object suffix <em>-anī</em> / <em>-an(y)</em> (and after
imperfect/imperative plural forms, <em>-ūnānī</em>, <em>-ūnāḵ</em>,
etc.).</p>
<ul>
<li>“He killed me”: Not <code>qṭal-y</code>, but
<strong>qaṭl-anī</strong>.</li>
<li>“He killed us”: <strong>qaṭl-an</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Vocalization Shifts &amp; 3rd Plural
Enclitics</strong></p>
<p>When adding a light (vowel-initial) object suffix to <em>qṭal</em>
(which has no vowel between the first two consonants: q-ṭ-a-l), the
vowel shifts back to separate the first two consonants (<em>qaṭl-</em>).
Before the heavy 2nd plural suffixes (<em>-kūn</em>, <em>-kēn</em>), the
stem retains its original vocalization (<em>qṭal-</em>). For 3rd person
plural objects (“them”), Classical Syriac does not attach
<code>-hūn</code> or <code>-hēn</code> to the verb; instead, it places
the enclitic pronouns <strong>ܐܢܘܢ</strong> (<em>ʾennōn</em>, m.pl.) and
<strong>ܐܢܝܢ</strong> (<em>ʾennēn</em>, f.pl.) immediately after the
verb.</p>
<ul>
<li><em>qṭal</em> + <em>-eh</em> <span class="math inline">→</span>
<strong>qaṭl-eh</strong> (q-a-ṭ-l-e-h, “he killed him”).</li>
<li><em>qṭal</em> + <em>ʾennōn</em> <span class="math inline">→</span>
<strong>qṭal ʾennōn</strong> (“he killed them [m.]”).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Meaning</strong></p>
<p><em>Qaṭleh</em> means “He killed him”.</p>
<ul>
<li>Subject: He (inherent in the base verb form).</li>
<li>Object: Him (the suffix).</li>
</ul>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Check 7</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Why does <i>qṭal</i> become <i>qaṭl-</i> before a suffix?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The suffix shifts the stress, moving the vowel forward.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The root changes.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The root stays q-ṭ-l.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[It becomes a Paael.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[There is no doubling.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[It is a spelling convention only.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The pronunciation changes.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Check 8</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[What buffer appears before the 1 c.sg. object suffix 'me'?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[A Nun: <i>qaṭl-anī</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[A Yodh: <i>qaṭl-ay</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[-ay is not the object suffix.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[A Taw: <i>qaṭl-tī</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[-t marks the subject.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[None: <i>qaṭl-ī</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The n buffer is needed.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="description"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Section 4: Verbal Suffix Paradigm (Root `q-ṭ-l`)</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 4: Verbal Suffix Paradigm (Root `q-ṭ-l`)</h3>
<p>Here is how the 3rd person masculine singular Perfect (<em>qṭal</em>,
“he killed”) takes pronominal direct objects.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 20%" />
<col style="width: 20%" />
<col style="width: 20%" />
<col style="width: 20%" />
<col style="width: 20%" />
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th style="text-align: left;">Object (English)</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Suffix / Enclitic Form</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Attached to <em>qṭal</em>
(Estrangela)</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Transliteration</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Translation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>me (1 c.sg.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܢܝ (-anī / -an(y))</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܢܝ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlanī</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed me</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>you (2 m.sg.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܟ (-āḵ)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܟ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlāḵ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed you (m)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>you (2 f.sg.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܟܝ (-ēḵ(y))</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܟܝ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlēḵ(y)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed you (f)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>him (3 m.sg.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܗ (-eh)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܗ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭleh</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed him</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>her (3 f.sg.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܗ (-āh)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܗ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlāh</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed her</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>us (1 c.pl.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܢ (-an)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlan</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed us</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>you (2 m.pl.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܟܘܢ (-kūn)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܟܘܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qṭalkūn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed you (mpl)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>you (2 f.pl.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ـܟܝܢ (-kēn)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠܟܝܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qṭalkēn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed you (fpl)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>them (3 m.pl.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܐܢܘܢ (ʾennōn)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠ ܐܢܘܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qṭal ʾennōn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed them (m)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>them (3 f.pl.)</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܐܢܝܢ (ʾennēn)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܩܛܠ ܐܢܝܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qṭal ʾennēn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he killed them (f)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><em>Note: Suffixes are also attached to Imperfect and Imperative
forms. On the 2 m.sg. Imperative, the 1 c.sg. suffix “-me” takes the
diphthongs <code>-aynī</code>: Peal ܦܪܘܩܝܢܝ (</em>pruqaynī<em>, “save
me!”), Paael of III-Yodh ܐܣܐܝܢܝ (</em>ʾassaynī<em>, “heal me!”). Compare
these with the 3 m.sg. Perfects ܦܪܩܢܝ (</em>parqanī<em>, “he saved me”)
and ܐܣܝܢܝ (</em>ʾassyanī<em>, “he healed me”).</em></p>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
